# Nahuatl Transcoder (Rust)

A Rust implementation of Edward H. Trager’s Nahuatl abugida transcoder. It converts text in traditional Latin orthography into the Private Use Area (PUA) encoding used by the NahuatlOne font.

The port aims to preserve the original C++ implementation’s behavior. Its output is validated against a golden corpus generated by the original transcoder.

## Features

- Classical Nahuatl → NahuatlOne abugida transcoder
- Classical Nahuatl → modern SEP orthography converter (`c2m`)
- Unicode-safe Rust implementation
- Command-line interface
- Corpus-based regression tests

## Build

```bash
cargo build --release
```

## Usage

Convert a text file, with each line processed independently:

```bash
cargo run -- words.txt
```

Or run the compiled binary:

```bash
./target/release/nahuatl_transcoder words.txt
```

## c2m

The repository includes a second command-line utility, `c2m`, which converts Classical Nahuatl orthography to modern SEP orthography.

Examples:

```text
quetzal  -> ketsal
ce       -> se
ci       -> si
cu       -> ku
huitzil  -> uitsil
```

Run it with:

```bash
cargo run --bin c2m -- words.txt
```

Or run the compiled binary:

```bash
./target/release/c2m words.txt
```

The converter:

- Normalizes accented and long vowels
- Converts classical spellings to modern SEP spellings
- Preserves the digraph `ch`

## Project layout

```text
src/
├── constants.rs
├── lib.rs
├── main.rs
├── structure.rs
└── bin/
    └── c2m.rs

tests/
└── golden.rs

golden.csv
words.txt
Cargo.toml
```

## Background

Nahuatl is a Uto-Aztecan language spoken in Mexico. Classical Nahuatl was written using the Latin alphabet after the arrival of the Spanish in the sixteenth century. This project ports an experimental abugida transcoder originally written in C++ by Edward H. Trager in 2016.

